    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This distinction between \"invalid\" and \"imperfect\" touches upon a crucial methodological debate. Proponents of the tests argue that their development strives for maximal predictive validity, offering a quantitative baseline for comparison where subjective judgment might fail. However, the sociological critique often moves beyond mere imperfection to question the very *premises* of what is being measured—suggesting that standardized metrics necessarily reduce complex human potential to a narrow, quantifiable set of discrete skills. By focusing on invalidity, scholars challenge whether a seemingly \"objective\" tool can ever be truly unbiased when applied within a deeply stratified social structure.",
